⟦2800⟧{{smaller block|<sup>1</sup> “Proetides implerunt falsis mugitibus agros.”—''Ecl.'' vi. 48.}}

⟦2800⟧{{smaller block|<sup>2</sup> Two explanations are offered by the Scholiast: either that the poet was “snub-nosed” (σιμός), or that he was the son of Simichus. The second is obviously a mere guess.}}

⟦2900⟧<sup>3</sup> τά που καὶ Ζηνὸς ἐπὶ θρόνον ἄγαγε φάμα, l. 93. It is possible that Zeus refers to Ptolemy: cf. Horace, ''Ep.'' i. 19, 43, ''Iovis auribus ista Servas'', where ''Iupiter'' = Augustus.

⟦3000⟧<sup>4</sup> Some think that there is an allusion to Apollonius Rhodius.

⟦3100⟧<sup>5</sup> Cf. Hiller, ''ad loc.''

